# Sibling ORM crates: use path deps in tracked Cargo.toml

The AIC PIM workspace lives under `c:\analog-pim\*` with each crate in
its own repo, and every contributor checks out the whole tree. The
`crates.pim.analoginfo.com` sparse registry is decommissioned (see
`pim-offline-server/AGENTS.md` "Release flow"). In that world, pinning
sibling-owned crates by git tag is the wrong tool: tag bumps require
coordinated changes across three repos (the producer, the forked
`mi3guyc/pim-orm` consumer, and the final consumer), lockfile drift
splits the graph into two versions of the same crate, and `cargo
update` hits the dead registry with 403 errors.

This rule pins the durable pattern.

## The rule

> **Cross-repo deps on team-owned sibling crates in `c:\analog-pim\*`
> use tracked `path = "../<crate>"` in the consumer's `Cargo.toml`,
> plus matching `[patch]` entries that redirect every transitive
> reference to the same path. Tag-pinned git deps are reserved for
> third-party / outside-of-workspace consumers.**

Concretely for `pim-offline-server` consuming `pim-orm-offline`:

```toml
# Cargo.toml — direct dep
[dependencies]
pim-orm-offline = { path = "../pim-orm-offline" }

# Cargo.toml — patch the URLs every transitive reference uses
[patch."sparse+https://crates.pim.analoginfo.com/"]
pim-orm-offline = { path = "../pim-orm-offline" }
# ... plus the other pim-orm-* tag pins for crates the team doesn't own

[patch."https://github.com/analoginfo-pim/pim-orm-offline"]
pim-orm-offline = { path = "../pim-orm-offline" }

[patch."https://github.com/analoginfo-pim/pim-orm"]
pim-orm = { git = "https://github.com/mi3guyc/pim-orm", branch = "fix/jsonb-bind-and-jobs-gate" }
```

The forked `mi3guyc/pim-orm` (which transitively depends on
`pim-orm-offline` by tag) is also patched, but its OWN `Cargo.toml`
keeps its tag pin so the fork builds standalone for its own CI. The
patches collapse the consumer's view to a single `path+...` source.

## Why this works

1. **One source URL for `pim-orm-offline`.** Both the direct dep and
 every transitive edge resolve to the same `path+` source. Cargo
 can't end up with two versions in the graph because there's only
 one identity.
2. **No `cargo update` against the dead registry.** Path deps are
 resolved locally on every `cargo check`. The lockfile records
 `path+...` entries with no version + no source-hash fields.
3. **No coordinated tag bumps.** Edit `pim-orm-offline` in place
 (the AGENTS.md "schema baseline policy" already permits this
 pre-release), and `cargo check` in `pim-offline-server` picks the
 change up immediately.
4. **Build reproducibility on fresh checkouts.** As long as
 `c:\analog-pim\pim-orm-offline` exists at HEAD, every contributor
 gets the same code. CI / build-server scripts that need a frozen
 snapshot can git-pin the producer repo at a SHA in their own
 setup script.

## Trade-off: third-party clones

A third party who clones only `pim-offline-server` without the
sibling tree gets `failed to read manifest at ../pim-orm-offline`.
That's the right failure for this team's distribution model: the
shipping artifact is the MSI / installer, not the source tarball.
If outside contributors ever need source-only builds, switch to a
git-tag dep at release-cut time (one-line manifest change) and
revert after.

## Forbidden constructs

- **Editing tracked `Cargo.toml` to `pim-orm-offline = { git = ...,
 tag = "vX.Y.Z" }`** as a way to "release" a change. Without a
 coordinated bump in the fork too, this re-introduces the
 dual-version trap. The path-dep pattern obsoletes the tag-bump
 dance entirely for this workspace.
- **Adding a path patch in gitignored `.cargo/config.toml` when the
 same patch belongs in tracked `Cargo.toml`.** The gitignored file
 is for *personal* overrides (a developer swapping a sibling crate
 to their own fork). Team-wide overrides go in `Cargo.toml` so
 every contributor gets them.
- **Dumping the whole `[patch."sparse+..."]` registry block** when
 the workspace still uses some non-team-owned crates by tag
 (`pim-orm`, `pim-orm-core`, etc., which the team doesn't own per
 `do-not-modify-upstream-orm.mdc`). Keep tag pins for those; only
 swap to path for team-owned crates (`pim-orm-offline` is the only
 one today).
- **Adding `Cargo.lock` to git just because path deps stabilize the
 lockfile.** The workspace `.gitignore` excludes `Cargo.lock` for a
 reason — letting contributors regenerate locally avoids merge
 conflicts on lockfile churn. Path deps don't change the calculus.

## When the dual-version graph DID appear (historical context)

Prior to this rule's enactment, the workspace used git-tag deps and
the dual-version trap fired twice in two days (2026-05-25 and
2026-05-26). Symptoms:

- `cargo check` emits `multiple different versions of crate
 pim_orm_offline` followed by ~7 `E0308 mismatched types` errors
 in handlers that pass commands across the version boundary.
- `cargo tree -i pim-orm-offline` shows two entries: a `0.3.14
 (https://...?tag=v0.3.14)` AND a `0.3.13
 (https://...?tag=v0.3.13)` (or whatever pair).
- `cargo update -p pim-orm` to refresh the fork's pin to a tag the
 fork has already bumped to fails with HTTP 403 from the dead
 sparse registry.
- Adding `[patch."https://github.com/analoginfo-pim/pim-orm-offline"]
 = { git = "...", tag = "v0.3.14" }` to side-step gives Cargo
 "patch points to the same source, but patches must point to
 different sources".

The path-dep pattern in this rule eliminates every one of these
failure modes by removing the version variable entirely.

## Recovery procedure if you find yourself in the trap

You're in the trap if `cargo tree -i pim-orm-offline` shows two
entries. To recover without resurrecting the registry:

1. Edit `pim-offline-server/Cargo.toml`: replace the direct
 `pim-orm-offline = { git = ..., tag = ... }` with `pim-orm-offline
 = { path = "../pim-orm-offline" }`.
2. Edit the `[patch."sparse+..."]` block: replace the
 `pim-orm-offline = { git = ..., tag = ... }` line with `pim-orm-offline
 = { path = "../pim-orm-offline" }`.
3. Add `[patch."https://github.com/analoginfo-pim/pim-orm-offline"]
 pim-orm-offline = { path = "../pim-orm-offline" }`.
4. Delete `Cargo.lock` (it's gitignored).
5. `cargo check --offline`. The lockfile regenerates with one
 `path+` entry and the build succeeds.

